  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1135 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nWednesday night through Sunday:\n\nAs the low pressure system moves east into Quebec, high pressure\nis expected to move down from the Northern Plains and Canada\ninto the Great Lakes Region. High pressure is expected to have\ninfluence across Wisconsin from roughly Wednesday night through\nFriday bringing dry weather, light winds and pleasant near\nnormal temperatures. \n\nHeading into the weekend, aloft (around 500 mb) zonal flow\nlooks to return as the ridge flattens out. This will place the\nGreat Lakes Region back into a regime of shortwave troughs \nmoving through the flow with multiple chances for rain and \nstorms. Rain and storms chances are expected to increase as \nearly as Saturday night with additional person of storms \nlooking to continue through Monday. Ahead of the first shortwave\ntrough late Saturday, winds are expected to shift to the south \nto southwest which will allow for some decent WAA. The moisture \nreturn should be enough to help support rain with any lift we \nget aloft. Global models are also still showing the LLJ moving \ninto Wisconsin which could further support storm development at \ntimes. At the end of the day, a lot  of the storm chances are \ngoing to be controlled by some mesoscale parameters that are \njust a bit to early have specifics on timing or exact location \nof rain/storms. What we do know is that, PWATS will be good \naround 1.5 to 2 inches which will promote heavy rain chances. \nInstability will be good at times, especially with the presence \nof the LLJ at times, and shear looks to be decent as well. So \nthere is support at the base level for ingredients for strong to\nsevere storms. However, do all of these ingredients line up at \nthe appropriate times. That will still need to be hashed out. \nFor planning purposes, this is not expected to be a washout on \nany given day. There will likely be many breaks or lulls between\nrounds of rain. So keep up to on the forecast. \n\nPatterson",
